A right-to-left glyph run drawn where DirectWrite puts it: its glyphs from its baseline origin leftward, each offset in
the run's direction, and the runs at their own origins (they come in visual order, so the index converter starts over
when a run's text comes before the text already read). Before, every run advanced to the right in glyph order, so an
Arabic or Hebrew word showed with its letters mirrored. GPUI's bidi layout (`build/patches/gpui-pre/0005`) puts the
glyphs into text order and back into visual order per row. A line whose first strong letter is right to left
(`gpui::first_strong_direction`, a leading right-to-left mark too) is laid out as a right-to-left paragraph, as the
other platforms' shapers do by themselves: else brackets and neutral characters took the left-to-right levels.
Its layout box is as wide as the line (DirectWrite refuses an infinite one there), so it starts at x = 0. The whole
line's first strong letter gives the direction of each part between its line separators. A tab takes its place from
the layout's hit test, as a right-to-left paragraph does not put it after the runs before it. The upstream test
`tabs_preserve_native_advances_and_cursor` reads x in text order, thus its Hebrew lines move to
`right_to_left_runs_keep_native_advances_in_visual_order`, which checks each glyph and tab in visual order. Our own.

